    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61547,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61548,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients may be symptomatic (secondary to mass effect) or asymptomatic. Ascites may be present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61549,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>Patients present over a wide age range (median: 43&nbsp;years), approximately a decade younger than those with high-grade serous carcinoma (HGSC). A small subset of patients have a prior history of a serous borderline tumour (SBT). LGSC accounts for approximately 5% of all ovarian carcinomas {20407318}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-05T17:44:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61550,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61551,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LGSC may exhibit <em>KRAS</em>, <em>NRAS</em>, <em>BRAF</em>, <em>USP9X</em>, and <em>EIF1AX</em> mutations {26506417}. High-stage tumours are less frequently associated with <em>BRAF</em> mutations. <em>KRAS</em> mutation is associated with tumour recurrence {24549645}. Loss of 9p and homozygous deletions of the <em>CDKN2A/2B</em> locus have also been reported {26506417}. A significant proportion of LGSCs arise in association with an SBT {31097112; 27487741}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61552,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LGSC is often bilateral and exhibits papillary growth. Tumours may be gritty due to calcification.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61553,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LGSC exhibits a variety of patterns, including small nests, glands, papillae or micropapillae, and inverted macropapillae&nbsp;&ndash; frequently free-floating within unlined clear spaces {27487741}. Often, several patterns of invasion are present in an individual tumour. The nuclei exhibit mild to moderate atypia, with &lt;&nbsp;3-fold variation in nuclear size, occasionally with a central nucleolus {15087669}. Mitotic figures are present, usually 1&ndash;2&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>, equating to 3&ndash;5&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.55&nbsp;mm in diameter and 0.24&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup> in area (with as many as 5&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>, equating to 12&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.55&nbsp;mm in diameter and 0.24&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup> in area) {15087669}. Psammoma bodies are often present. Necrosis is rare. These tumours are frequently associated with a coexisting SBT {31097112; 27487741}. Tumours are typically diffusely positive for CK7, PAX8, ER, and WT1; p16 expression is patchy and p53 exhibits wildtype immunoreactivity {21716157; 29901523}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61554,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LGSC cannot be distinguished from SBT on the basis of cytological features. Additionally, both entities typically exhibit wildtype p53 expression and lack block-positive p16 (<em>CDKN2A</em>) {23558569}. Fluid specimens involved by serous tumours with low-grade nuclear atypia are best characterized as &ldquo;involved by serous neoplasm&rdquo;, with definitive classification deferred to an excisional specimen.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61555,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61556,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> invasive serous tumour with small nests, glands, papillae or micropapillae, and inverted macropapillae, frequently free-floating within unlined clear spaces; low-grade cytological atypia (&lt;&nbsp;3-fold variation in nuclear size); low mitotic activity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T14:44:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61557,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>LGSC is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:01:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61558,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 12,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>LGSC is associated with a more indolent clinical course than HGSC {16880307}. When confined to the ovary, LGSC has an excellent prognosis, but long-term follow-up indicates that the prognosis for patients with stage&nbsp;III&ndash;IV disease is poor {26195696; 24071867}. For patients with stage&nbsp;II&ndash;IV&nbsp;disease, the median progression-free and overall survival times, respectively, are 28 and 100&nbsp;months {26195696}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T13:17:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]